    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d387">

                <pb facs="0575" n="499" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg499"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">368.1121 Stephano, Christopher/12</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Greece</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 185</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-05-03">May 3, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received your despatch
                    No. 594, of April 5, 1935,<anchor n="8" xml:id="d387-f1"/> reporting the release
                    of Mr. Christopher Stephano, who had been under arrest on the charge of high
                    treason in connection with the recent rebellion in Greece. The effective manner
                    in which you and your staff dealt with this case meets with the Department’s
                    commendation.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
